{"name":"Xiān Hóng Red","hex":"#e60626","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Siam Confederation (Xiān)","blocks":[{"text":"Xiān, rendered in Thai as Siam, is the name by which Chinese and Vietnamese records of the 12th to 14th centuries designated one or more polities in the lower Chao Phraya basin. Rather than a single centralised kingdom, Yoneo Ishii proposed that Chinese usage covered several maritime-oriented port polities around the present Bay of Bangkok, naming Ayodhya, Suphan Buri and probably Ligor. Phrip Phri sent embassies to the Yuan court in its own name, and Nakhon Si Thammarat was reckoned as Siamese from the late 13th century. These polities emerged on the western Chao Phraya plain after the decline of Dvaravati in the 11th century and are treated as successors of Qiān Zhī Fú at Si Thep and related states."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Lower_Menam_Valley_in_the_13th_century.png/330px-Lower_Menam_Valley_in_the_13th_century.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siam_Confederation_(Xi%C4%81n)"},{"title":"Xi'an","blocks":[{"text":"Xi'an is the capital of the Chinese province of Shaanxi. A sub-provincial city on the Guanzhong plain, the city is the most populous in the province and third-most populous in Western China after Chongqing and Chengdu, as well as the most populous in Northwestern China. Based on the city's 2025 1% population sample survey, its estimated year-end resident population was 13.24 million, of whom 10.72 million were urban residents."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/City_wall_of_Xi%27an_51550-Xian_%2827959363326%29.jpg/330px-City_wall_of_Xi%27an_51550-Xian_%2827959363326%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi'an"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Siamese_Mercenaries_Angkor_Wat_0876.jpg/500px-Siamese_Mercenaries_Angkor_Wat_0876.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Siamese_Mercenaries_Angkor_Wat_0876.jpg/1280px-Siamese_Mercenaries_Angkor_Wat_0876.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Siamese_Mercenaries_Angkor_Wat_0876.jpg/500px-Siamese_Mercenaries_Angkor_Wat_0876.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Relief at Angkor Wat, dated to the early 12th century, showing Siamese mercenaries led by a noble of Cheng Shan (เชงฌาล), a city that later became a major rival of Angkor.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASiamese_Mercenaries_Angkor_Wat_0876.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Ngoenyang_1156-1180.png/330px-Ngoenyang_1156-1180.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Ngoenyang_1156-1180.png/330px-Ngoenyang_1156-1180.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Approximate greatest extent of Tai Yuan Ngoenyang following Chueang's southern campaign in the 1150s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANgoenyang_1156-1180.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Political_network_depicted_in_the_Tamnan_Fa_Daet_Song_Yang.png/500px-Political_network_depicted_in_the_Tamnan_Fa_Daet_Song_Yang.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Political_network_depicted_in_the_Tamnan_Fa_Daet_Song_Yang.png/500px-Political_network_depicted_in_the_Tamnan_Fa_Daet_Song_Yang.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Interpretive reconstruction of the political network described in the Tamnan Fa Daet Song Yang, showing the Northern Alliance led by Muang Chiang Som (brown), the rival Southern Alliance led by Muang Fa Daet (blue), and neighboring contemporary polities (black).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APolitical_network_depicted_in_the_Tamnan_Fa_Daet_Song_Yang.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Suvarnapura.jpg/500px-Suvarnapura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Suvarnapura.jpg/500px-Suvarnapura.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c